    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Chapter 10: Basic Macroeconomic Relationships\"\nsubtitle: \"Practice Problems\"\n---\n\n## Warm-Up: Quick Concept and Calculation Checks\n\na. In Year 1, Priya's disposable income is $35,000 and she consumes $32,000. In Year 2, her\n   disposable income rises to $40,000 and she consumes $35,600. What is her MPC out of that\n   $5,000 raise?\nb. Using your answer to (a), what is Priya's MPS out of that raise?\nc. For each situation, say whether it causes a *movement along* the consumption schedule or a\n   *shift* of the whole schedule, and if a shift, which direction: \n   (i) disposable income rises,\n   (ii) a booming stock market makes households feel wealthier, (iii) banks make credit cards\n   easier to get, (iv) real interest rates rise sharply, encouraging more saving.\nd. A firm is considering four possible investment projects with these expected rates of return:\n   Project W, 10 percent; Project X, 8 percent; Project Y, 4 percent; Project Z, 2 percent. If the\n   real interest rate is 5 percent, which projects should the firm undertake?\ne. True or False: investment spending tends to be more stable than consumption spending over the\n   business cycle.\nf. If the MPS rises, does the simple spending multiplier ($1/MPS$) get larger or smaller?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show Answer</summary>\n\na. $MPC = \\Delta C / \\Delta DI = (35{,}600 - 32{,}000)/(40{,}000-35{,}000) = 3{,}600/5{,}000 = 0.72$.\nb. $MPS = 1 - MPC = 1 - 0.72 = 0.28$.\nc. (i) *Movement along* the schedule. (ii) *Shift up* (the wealth effect increases consumption at\n   every income level). (iii) *Shift up* (easier credit supports more consumption at every income\n   level). (iv) *Shift down* in consumption, *shift up* in saving (higher real rates reward saving\n   over spending at every income level).\nd. Undertake any project whose expected return *exceeds* the 5 percent real interest rate:\n   *Project W (10 percent) and Project X (8 percent)*. Projects Y and Z do not clear the 5 percent\n   hurdle.\ne. *False.* Investment is considerably *more* volatile than consumption, since capital purchases\n   can be postponed and profit expectations shift quickly.\nf. *Smaller.* A higher MPS means more of each new dollar of income leaks into saving rather than\n   being re-spent, so each round of the spending chain is smaller and the total multiplied effect\n   is smaller.\n\n</details>\n\n## Problem 1: Investment Demand and the Real Interest Rate\n\nA small city has five local businesses each considering one investment project:\n\n| Business | Project | Expected Rate of Return |\n|---|---|---|\n| Bakery | New oven | 14% |\n| Garage | Second work bay | 11% |\n| Cafe | Outdoor seating | 8% |\n| Bookstore | Storefront renovation | 6% |\n| Gym | New equipment | 3% |\n\na. If the real interest rate is 4 percent, which projects get undertaken?\nb. Suppose the real interest rate instead *rises* to 9 percent. Which project(s), if any, no\n   longer clear the hurdle and get dropped?\nc. Explain, in your own words, why a *rising* real interest rate decreases the total quantity of\n   investment demanded in an economy.\nd. Name two factors, other than the interest rate, that could shift the entire investment demand\n   curve to the right (more investment demanded at every interest rate).\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show Answer</summary>\n\n::: {#58208061 .cell execution_count=1}\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}\n```{=html}\n<div style=\"height:400px; width:560px;\">            <script src=\"https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/mathjax/2.7.5/MathJax.js?config=TeX-AMS-MML_SVG\"></script><script>if (window.MathJax && window.MathJax.Hub && window.MathJax.Hub.Config) {window.MathJax.Hub.Config({SVG: {font: \"STIX-Web\"}});}</script>                <script>window.PlotlyConfig = {MathJaxConfig: 'local'};</script>\n        <script charset=\"utf-8\" src=\"https://cdn.plot.ly/plotly-3.7.0.min.js\" integrity=\"sha256-jvTGqxNp8AGWEcvNLVuKr+8j5dGe9