(outputEl, {childList: true});\n}}\n\n                        })                };            </script>        </div>\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\na. At 4 percent: the *Bakery (14%)*, *Garage (11%)*, *Cafe (8%)*, and *Bookstore (6%)* projects\n   all clear the hurdle; only the *Gym (3%)* does not.\nb. At 9 percent: only the *Bakery (14%)* and *Garage (11%)* still clear the hurdle. The *Cafe\n   (8%)* and *Bookstore (6%)* projects now fall below it and get dropped.\nc. As the real interest rate rises, borrowing to fund a project becomes more expensive, so\n   projects whose expected return no longer exceeds that higher cost become unprofitable and get\n   shelved. This is exactly why the investment demand curve slopes downward: a higher real\n   interest rate reduces the total quantity of investment demanded.\nd. Any two of: improving technology that raises expected returns, falling costs of acquiring or\n   operating capital, lower business taxes, or more optimistic business expectations about future\n   demand.\n\n</details>\n\n## Problem 2: The Multiplier in Action\n\nA new employer moves to town and immediately spends $80 million building a facility. Suppose the\nMPC in this local economy is 0.6.\n\na. What is the MPS?\nb. What is the simple spending multiplier?\nc. What is the total eventual change in local income from this initial $80 million in spending?\nd. Show the first four rounds of the spending chain (the initial spending, then each subsequent\n   round of re-spending).\ne. If local residents instead had an MPC of only 0.4, would the total impact on local income be\n   larger or smaller than in part (c)? Explain in one sentence.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show Answer</summary>\n\n::: {#178ad91b .cell execution_count=2}\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}\n```{=html}\n<div style=\"height:420px; width:620px;\">            <script src=\"https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/mathjax/2.7.5/MathJax.js?config=TeX-AMS-MML_SVG\"></script><script>if (window.MathJax && 